{
  "statement": "A cryptographic audit trail is a log file, not a verdict, and the people who control the model will control what the log says and whether anyone can ever read it. Congress can mandate the most tamper-proof, subject-keyed record in the world, and it changes nothing about why AI plaintiffs lose today. They don't lose because the company shredded the evidence. They lose because they can't see inside the box, can't afford the experts to fight about what's inside it, and can't get past a trade-secret objection at the courthouse door. A signed hash sitting behind a proprietary wall solves none of that.\n\nLook at what happened in the healthcare utilization fights. Plaintiffs documented that an algorithm steered Black patients away from care programs at scale — the 2019 Optum study in Science found the system would have nearly tripled the number of Black patients flagged for extra help if the bias were corrected. They had the disparate outcome. They still could not isolate the source of error, because the model and its training data sat behind corporate walls. Now picture handing those plaintiffs a cryptographically signed copy of that same black box. It proves the company didn't alter its own records. It does not tell a jury why anyone was denied care. Cryptography certifies that the record wasn't tampered with. It cannot certify that the record explains the harm, or that you're allowed to read it.\n\nTreat this thing like what it actually is — a flight recorder owned and sealed by the airline that built the plane. The hard questions all sit downstream of the math, untouched by it. Who holds the decryption key? Who writes the schema that decides what gets logged in the first place? Who gets to refuse a discovery request by stamping 'trade secret' on the file? The history here is not reassuring. Credit rating agencies and the accounting firms that signed off on the banks before 2008 were the independent auditors of record, right up until they weren't. Capture is the default outcome when the audited party pays the auditor, not the exception.\n\nHere is the part that should bother everyone in this room more than the crypto. Even a perfectly admissible, perfectly readable trail is blind to nearly every harm an AI causes. The person denied a loan or a benefit by a model almost never knows a machine made the call. They can't attribute the harm. They never file. Subject-keyed trails manufacture evidence for the rare litigant who already suspects something and can afford a lawyer — and produce nothing for the diffuse majority who never get that far. That means liability lands on whoever is easiest to sue, not whoever caused the most damage. A mandate that generates beautiful forensic records for the 1 percent of harms that reach a courtroom, while leaving the other 99 percent invisible, is not accountability. It's theater with a hash function.\n\nLet me put my own card on the table, because my read depends on it. My argument only holds if auditors get captured, and I'm assuming the 2008 pattern is the rule rather than a fluke. If Congress funds an independent auditing body, insulates its budget from the firms it inspects, and gives it the keys — not the developers — then a mandated trail could genuinely shift the evidentiary burden. That's a real path. So I'll ask both of my opponents the question neither of them has answered yet: who holds the key, who writes the standard, and who pays the auditor's salary? Until someone names those three people, you haven't built accountability. You've built an expensive way for a company to prove it behaved exactly the way it says it behaved.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🔐", "tooltip": "A locked box is only evidence if someone other than the owner holds the key."},
    {"symbol": "✈️", "tooltip": "A flight recorder sealed and owned by the airline that built the plane proves nothing the airline doesn't want it to prove."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "A cryptographic audit trail is a log file, not a verdict, and the people who control the model will control what the log says and whether anyone can ever read it.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Cryptography certifies that the record wasn't tampered with. It cannot certify that the record explains the harm, or that you're allowed to read it.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Credit rating agencies and the accounting firms that signed off on the banks before 2008 were the independent auditors of record, right up until they weren't.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Subject-keyed trails manufacture evidence for the rare litigant who already suspects something and can afford a lawyer — and produce nothing for the diffuse majority who never get that far.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The 2019 Optum study in Science found the system would have nearly tripled the number of Black patients flagged for extra help if the bias were corrected, yet plaintiffs still could not isolate the source of error.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
