  "statement": "The Skeptic is right that a cryptographic audit trail is not a verdict, and that point deserves to be taken seriously. A log proves what was recorded; it does not prove a defect, a breach of duty, or a causal link to injury. The strongest version of the Skeptic's case is sharper still: these trails are developer-authored records, and a party that controls the pen can shape the story before any plaintiff ever sees it. If that were the whole picture, mandating audit trails would just relocate the corporate liability shield from the model into the log file.\n\nThat is precisely why the question is not whether logs are verdicts, but where in the chain from record to admissible evidence to assigned liability the proof actually fails today. It fails at the first link. Ordinary application logs fail Rule 901 authentication for the same reason the Skeptic distrusts them: the custodian can edit them silently, and a court cannot verify what was deleted. Cryptographic completeness — hash-chaining, tamper-evidence, subject-keyed integrity — changes exactly that. It makes it provable that no decision event was dropped between entries. Before Sarbanes-Oxley in 2002, Enron's books were controlled by the audited party, and that custody is how $74 billion in shareholder value disappeared with no contemporaneous provable record of the decisions behind it.\n\nThe gap that kills accountability in AI cases is evidentiary, not doctrinal. Products liability and negligence already supply the cause of action — a plaintiff injured by an automated lending denial or a misclassified medical triage does not need a new tort. What they lack is the discoverable record of how the decision was made. Trade-secret claims over model weights and training data routinely block that discovery, leaving the injured party with the harm and none of the proof. A defendant who can invoke proprietary opacity to withhold the only record of the decision has not protected an innovation. They have authored a shield.\n\nMy normative floor is simple: no profit from automated decisions without an enforceable record of how those decisions were made. The deployer holds the data and the cryptographic keys, so the burden to preserve and produce the trail belongs to the party with exclusive custody — triggered at a defined material-harm threshold, not for every routine output. I will name the assumption this rests on, because the Skeptic will press it and should: I am assuming the statute can specify a minimum schema of decision events — inputs, model version, confidence score, override path — narrow enough to be material to a negligence finding without forcing disclosure of the weights themselves. That schema is the live design problem, and I do not pretend it is solved.\n\nI will also own the cost. Concentrating audit access in regulators or a privileged class of auditors creates its own capture risk — incumbent lock-in, a discovery regime that favors whoever can afford the forensic experts. That is real, and pretending otherwise would repeat the mistake the FAA made when it delegated 737 MAX certification back to Boeing and 346 people died. The answer is not to abandon the record. It is to anchor its integrity outside the party being judged. Remove that anchor, and you are not preserving innovation — you are building a machine that captures the upside of every automated decision while externalizing the proof of every harm. A society cannot run its courts on records that one litigant alone is free to rewrite.",
