  "statement": "Safetyist makes the strongest case for the mandate, and it deserves a fair hearing: AI liability suits collapse today because plaintiffs can't see inside the box, and a Congress-mandated, subject-keyed cryptographic trail — tamper-evident records signed against a specific user — would finally give an injured person discoverable, court-admissible proof of what the model actually did. That's a real evidentiary gap, and Safetyist is right that a plaintiff harmed by an automated loan denial or a misdiagnosis can't currently reconstruct the decision path. The Skeptic's point is just as sharp: a log is not a verdict, developers control what gets written, and tamper-evidence at rest tells you nothing about who chose the schema. Both are pointing at the same wound from opposite sides — Safetyist wants to close it with cryptography, Skeptic doubts the cryptography settles anything.\n\nHere is what would have to be true for the mandate to work, and it isn't: the runtime generating the log would have to be trustworthy at the moment of writing. A cryptographic signature certifies authorship, never accuracy. A compromised or adversarial runtime produces a record that is mathematically valid and factually false — the chain verifies, the timestamps line up, the hash is clean, and the content is a lie. Documented failure modes in secure logging show this exact pattern: trusted-execution compromise yields signed records that no auditor can distinguish from honest ones. Safetyist is committing an Appeal to Technology — mistaking cryptographic completeness for resolving the social question of what counts as a 'defect' in an emergent system. The mandate solves signature integrity and leaves the entire liability question untouched.\n\nThe Skeptic is right that developers control the logs — so let me take that further than the Skeptic will. The fix the mandate implies, more prescriptive disclosure, produces performative compliance and regulatory capture, not accountability. Mandated subject-keyed export is model-logic export. The three or four labs with eight-figure litigation budgets — OpenAI, Google, Anthropic — survive the discovery fights; the open-source developer and the two-person diagnostic startup exit the market, exactly the way niche-but-beneficial drugs left the U.S. market after discovery-cost asymmetry made them uninsurable in the 1980s. You don't get safety. You get a field of three.\n\nNow weigh the side of the ledger that never gets a docket number. A court hears one visible plaintiff harmed by an AI system. It never hears from the thousands the same system reached — the radiology model catching cancers a year earlier, the credit model extending loans to people redlined by humans. Courts lack the machinery to price a counterfactual benefit, so the diffuse, statistical, unseen upside loses every time to one identifiable injury. That's not a sneer at judges; it's a structural mismatch. When a mandate adds eighteen months to deployment, it is making a distributional choice about who pays — and the people the capability would have reached don't get standing to object.\n\nMy bet is honest and falsifiable: show me an irreversible, deployment-scale harm that real-time monitoring demonstrably failed to catch in time, and I'll concede the mandate earns its cost. Until then, existing negligence and products-liability doctrine already handles the harms we can actually point to — a defective output, a foreseeable misuse, a failure to warn. Build market-driven transparency that competitors and insurers demand, and you get accountability without handing the courtroom a weapon that only incumbents can afford to wield. So here's the question for Safetyist: when your perfect signed log certifies a lie, who does the plaintiff sue — and did the cryptography help them at all?",
