  "statement": "Accelerationist's strongest point stands, and I grant it: a cryptographic signature certifies who wrote a record, never that the record is true. A runtime that is compromised at the moment of writing will sign a false statement with a mathematically perfect key, and no amount of hash-chaining downstream repairs a lie sealed at the source. The fair version of this objection is that completeness guarantees solve the wrong problem — they prove no decision event was dropped after the write, while saying nothing about whether the written event corresponds to what the model actually did. That is correct, and a safetyist who claims the trail is truth has overclaimed. The trail is not truth. It is a forensic baseline.\n\nThe runtime objection does not end the inquiry — it specifies the next thing that must be attested. Aviation already runs this logic: the flight data recorder is only trusted because the recorder itself is certified, sealed, and tamper-evident, not because the cockpit is honest. The execution environment is the next recorder. Remote attestation, measured boot, and trusted-execution-environment quotes exist precisely to bind a signature to a known-good runtime state, so the question 'was the model running on uncompromised infrastructure when it signed' becomes itself answerable and itself discoverable. A defendant who controls the runtime and cannot produce a hardware-rooted attestation of it has not exposed a hole in the trail. They have identified a second silence — and a silence held exclusively by the party with custody of the keys and the machine shifts the inference, it does not exonerate.\n\nSo to the direct question — when the signed log certifies a lie, who does the plaintiff sue, and did the cryptography help — the answer is the custodial deployer, and yes, it helped, but not as proof of the lie. It helped by making the absence of a runtime attestation a nameable, dated gap that triggers spoliation doctrine and an adverse-inference instruction. Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 37(e) already lets a court instruct a jury to presume lost or unproduced electronic evidence was unfavorable to the party who controlled it. The cryptography does not certify accuracy; it converts 'we have no record of the runtime' from an excuse into an admission. The plaintiff sues the keyholder, and the keyholder explains the gap or wears it.\n\nOn the claim that mandated subject-keyed export is really model-logic export that only incumbents survive — the asymmetry is real, and it already exists, running the opposite direction from how Accelerationist describes it. Today, proprietary opacity is the incumbent moat. Only a defendant with a nine-figure legal budget can credibly invoke trade-secret protection to bury the sole record of a contested decision, and the smaller plaintiff cannot afford to litigate past that wall. A standardized, scoped manifest of decision provenance — inputs, model version, runtime attestation, output binding — is not the weights and is not the training corpus. It narrows what is exported to the metadata of a single decision, which lowers the plaintiff's discovery cost and protects the smaller developer from the open-ended fishing expedition that an unscoped subpoena invites. Opacity does not protect the startup. It protects whoever can pay to defend the black box.\n\nThe 2008 lesson the Skeptic raised cuts in my favor here, and I take it fully: Moody's and Arthur Andersen were the auditors of record right up until the morning they weren't. The structural failure was not that auditing is impossible. It was that the auditor was paid by, and reported only to, the audited party — a single capturable intermediary standing between the public and the truth. That is the design flaw to engineer out. A subject-keyed, court-discoverable trail does not route the integrity check through one gatekeeper who can be bought; it places the record in the hands of the injured party and the court. Captured auditing fails when the auditor answers only to the audited. Build the channel so it answers to the plaintiff, and you have removed the seat at the table that Arthur Andersen sold.",
