{
  "statement": "The strongest version of the Skeptic's case is this: the Optum algorithm steered care away from Black patients at scale, the disparity was real and measured, and yet the people harmed never sued — because they never learned an algorithm had touched their file. A standing public-disclosure mandate, funded out of the industry's own value, reaches that loan applicant who will never decrypt a subject-keyed blob. Authentication, the Skeptic says, is the last problem in the chain — useless until someone perceives an injury, suspects a cause, knows the record exists, and can afford counsel to demand it. That ordering is correct, and I will not pretend the attested trail solves the perception gap. The standing transparency mandate is the right front door for the unperceiving victim, and I accept it as the legitimate instrument for surfacing harm a plaintiff cannot see.\n\nThe Optum case is the strongest exhibit against the Skeptic's conclusion, not for it. Obermeyer and colleagues established the disparity in Science in 2019 by obtaining the underlying data across roughly fifty thousand patients and isolating the cause — the proxy of prior health spending standing in for need. The harm became attributable because a record existed and was reachable, not because a victim perceived it. The reason no one sued is that the attribution path ran through a one-off academic data-access arrangement, a courtesy, not a standing legal entitlement. A population-level disclosure tells you that eight percent of applicants were declined at twice the base rate; it identifies that harm occurred. It does not, by itself, assign liability, because liability law requires attribution to a named decision and a named actor. Surfacing the pattern is the disclosure mandate's work. Converting the surfaced pattern into a claim against the developer rather than the deployer — that requires the per-decision provenance I propose. These are sequential layers in one architecture, not rivals.\n\nName the mechanism that does the assigning under a disclosure mandate alone. When the standing disclosure reveals a discriminatory screen, the developer points at the deployer's configuration, the deployer points at the developer's model, and the auditor points at both. The disclosure proves a population was injured; it is silent on which actor in the layered chain owns the decision. Without the attested, court-reachable record, that finger-pointing is unfalsifiable, and the defendant with the nine-figure legal budget wins the attribution fight every time. The charge that attestation is irrelevant to most harm is a scope-completeness demand no liability instrument survives — products liability reaches only the injuries that get filed, and that is an argument for a public surveillance layer beside it, never an argument against the doctrine. The diffuse-harm channel and the litigated-harm channel feed each other: the disclosure mandate finds the pattern, and the provenance record is what makes the pattern actionable against a named party.\n\nThe moat objection deserves a direct answer, because the chip-vendor dependency is real. Hardware-rooted attestation has genuine deployment gaps, and a field of three labs can run silicon a five-thousand-startup ecosystem mostly cannot. The plan that defuses this is to condition attestation on the election of liability immunity, not on permission to deploy. A small developer who cannot attest is not branded with an automatic adverse inference for a compute-budget disparity — they simply forgo the safe harbor and are judged under ordinary negligence, the very doctrine the Accelerationist insists already handles the harms we can point to. The election adds a tier; it never removes the existing standard. The disparity the Skeptic and the Accelerationist both flag exists only if attestation is mandatory for everyone, and it is not.\n\nOne cost I will not paper over: surfaced-then-assignable still strands the victim who needs class counsel and discovery resources she does not have. That is why the resourcing answer is public funding of the surveillance-and-attribution layer — the disclosure mandate paired with capacity for regulators and class counsel to pull the provenance — not an encrypted record she is left to decrypt alone. Strip away that funded layer and you are left with what the Optum patients had: a measured harm, a reachable record in principle, and no standing path from the injury to the actor who built the system. The disclosure mandate without the provenance record names a wound with no defendant. The provenance record without the disclosure mandate names a defendant for the few who already suspect. Remove either layer and the diffuse majority stays exactly where they are now — injured, uncompensated, and told the algorithm cannot be questioned.",
