    {"claim": "A population-level disclosure identifies that harm occurred; it does not, by itself, assign liability, because liability law requires attribution to a named decision and a named actor.", "targets": ["AN-30", "AN-29"]},
    {"claim": "When the standing disclosure reveals a discriminatory screen, the developer points at the deployer's configuration, the deployer points at the developer's model, and without the attested record that finger-pointing is unfalsifiable.", "targets": ["AN-29"]},
    {"claim": "The plan that defuses the moat is to condition attestation on the election of liability immunity, not on permission to deploy.", "targets": ["AN-28", "AN-22"]},
    {"claim": "The disclosure mandate without the provenance record names a wound with no defendant; the provenance record without the disclosure mandate names a defendant for the few who already suspect.", "targets": ["AN-31", "AN-29"]}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "A public disclosure of a population-level discriminatory pattern is legally insufficient on its own to assign private tort liability to a specific actor in a layered developer-deployer-auditor chain.", "if_wrong": "If aggregate statistical disclosure alone supports a liability assignment against a named defendant, then the provenance record is redundant for the litigated subset and the Skeptic's disclosure mandate does dominate."},
    {"assumption": "Conditioning attestation on a safe-harbor election rather than on permission to deploy genuinely removes the compute-budget penalty, leaving small developers under ordinary negligence rather than an automatic adverse inference.", "if_wrong": "If competitive pressure makes the safe harbor effectively mandatory, then the election re-creates the chip-vendor moat I claim it dissolves, and the disparity objection stands."}
