  "statement": "Here is the strongest version of the Safetyist's pipeline, stated the way they'd want it stated: ordinary application logs are worthless in court because the custodian can edit them silently, so you hash-chain the trail to prove no decision event was dropped; then you root the runtime in measured boot or a trusted execution environment — a secure enclave whose state is cryptographically attested — so that when a defendant cannot produce that attestation, the missing record stops being an innocent gap and becomes a culpable silence the court can punish with an adverse-inference instruction under the spoliation rule. That's a genuinely clever move. It converts 'we have no record' from a shrug into an admission, and it puts the trail in the hands of the injured party instead of a captured auditor. I'll grant the first link outright: editable logs fail authentication, and hash-chaining fixes that. Take it.\n\nThe move breaks at the silicon. The Safetyist already conceded the attestation root itself can fail — and that concession is fatal to the adverse-inference theory, because the inference only fires if the court treats the ABSENCE of attestation as culpable rather than as a hardware-budget gap. Walk the regress: if the runtime is untrustworthy, the attestation is untrustworthy; if the attestation can be untrustworthy, then a missing attestation is not proof of bad faith — it's just a missing attestation. The same authorship-not-accuracy problem I flagged at the log layer recurs one floor down at the trust root. A compromised enclave signs a measurement that is cryptographically perfect and factually false, exactly as a compromised runtime signs an intent statement that is valid and deceptive. You have not closed the gap. You have relocated it from the courtroom to the foundry and added a hardware dependency on top.\n\nNow count who clears that bar. A measured-boot, attested-enclave supply chain is not what a four-person team shipping a triage model on commodity or open infrastructure has. So when the spoliation rule asks 'where is your attestation,' the incumbent with a secure-enclave deployment answers and the small developer who couldn't afford the silicon eats an adverse inference — not for any harm they caused, but for a compute-budget disparity. Show me the drafted safe-harbor language that distinguishes 'destroyed the record' from 'never could afford the attestation infrastructure.' It doesn't exist in any of these proposals. Until it does, the pipeline punishes the developer who lacks a trusted-execution supply chain and rewards the one who can document compliance — which is the incumbent moat wearing a leveler's costume.\n\nThe leveling claim only survives if you keep two things apart that the Safetyist welded together. A standardized provenance manifest — the data format, the schema of what gets recorded — may genuinely be cheap to emit and may genuinely shrink a plaintiff's discovery cost. Grant that layer. But the manifest is court-admissible under their own theory only because the attestation root vouches for it, and that root is not cheap and not commodity in the way that matters. So name the certifier: who attests the attestation? If the answer is a hardware vendor or a designated attestation authority, you have rebuilt the single capturable gatekeeper the subject-keyed trail was supposed to abolish — just etched into silicon this time, where it's harder to audit and impossible to fork. And if the answer is 'the cloud provider's TPM,' then the record of every contested decision now lives behind the platform whose acceptable-use terms give the harmed individual no standing to demand it.\n\nHere's the cost nobody at this table has priced. Completeness is not correctness — a perfectly hash-chained, perfectly attested trail proves no event was dropped and proves nothing about whether the recorded events describe what the model actually did, so the rare funded plaintiff who reaches discovery still litigates authorship from a record that only certifies its own custody. Meanwhile every month the attestation bar keeps an open or small developer out of a deployable diagnostic tool, patients wait on the legacy system that misses what the model would have caught. The 2019 Optum finding the Skeptic raised cuts both ways: the bias was real, and the people it failed were already inside the deployed system — not protected by its absence. Who gave the foundry the right to decide which triage tools are allowed to exist?",
