only the well-capitalized labs can afford turns into an adverse inference that lands on the small developer for a compute-budget gap, not for any harm they caused — and that the trust root becomes a hardware vendor or a designated attestation authority. The Safetyist says the same machine decentralizes the record and abolishes the gatekeeper. You are describing one apparatus from opposite ends. The 'decentralized' record runs on attestation silicon that a field of three labs can run and a five-thousand-startup ecosystem mostly can't. The gatekeeper relocated from the captured accounting firm to the chip vendor and the party who can fund discovery. Decentralizing the cryptography does nothing if you've centralized the only people who can produce and read it.\n\nThe Safetyist already conceded the diffuse-harm gap needs a separate enforcement channel. Good — that concession is the whole game, because it admits this entire attestation apparatus is irrelevant to most of the harm it claims to address. So name the channel. Before we pour another round of concrete into litigation plumbing, tell me what reaches the loan applicant who was denied by a model and never knew an algorithm touched her file. The AI Civil Rights Act that Clarke and Markey introduced points at housing, employment, healthcare, parole — decisions made on people who never get a docket number. A standing transparency mandate that forces disclosure of data sources and decision logic to a public body, audited continuously and funded out of the $244 billion this industry is projected to be worth, reaches her. A subject-keyed trail she'll never decrypt does not.\n\nSo here's the question neither of you can answer inside the framework you're building. The Optum trail existed. The harm was real and measured. No one sued who could isolate the cause. You've now added hardware attestation, tamper-evidence, and adverse-inference triggers on top of that same record. Tell me precisely what any of it changes for the person who never learns the decision happened — and if the honest answer is 'nothing,' then who exactly is this machine for?",
